CSE 550 Problem Set 2 — Bufferbloat
====================================

Reproduction
------------
Environment: Ubuntu 20.04 Mininet VM (Linux 5.4.0-42-generic, Python 3.8).

The skeleton code assumed Python 2 and the tcp_probe kernel module (removed in
Linux 4.16+). Scripts were ported to Python 3, tcp_probe was replaced by
polling "ss -i dst :5001" every 100 ms inside h1's network namespace, and curl
was replaced by wget (not installed on the VM image).

To run both experiments (q=20 and q=100) and produce all six plots:

    sudo ./run.sh

Answers to Questions
--------------------

Q1. Why do you see a difference in webpage fetch times with short and large
    router buffers?

    With a large buffer (q=100) the long-lived iperf flow fills the queue before
    TCP backs off, forcing short HTTP fetches to wait behind the backlog on every
    RTT (~800 ms extra queuing delay per round trip), stretching fetch times to
    ~8.6 s on average. With a small buffer (q=20) maximum queuing delay is
    ~160 ms per queue, so short flows finish in ~2.7 s on average.

Q2. What is the transmit queue length on the NIC reported by ifconfig?
    For a 100 Mb/s drain rate, what is the maximum wait time?

    ifconfig eth0 reports txqueuelen 1000.
    Maximum wait = 1000 × 1500 B × 8 / 100,000,000 b/s = 0.12 s (120 ms).

Q3. How does the RTT reported by ping vary with queue size?
    Give a symbolic equation.

        RTT(q) = RTT_prop + 2 × q × (MTU × 8 / C)

    RTT_prop = 20 ms (minimum RTT), MTU = 1500 B, C = 1.5 Mb/s, q = queue
    occupancy in packets. The factor of 2 arises because TCLink places the
    rate-limited buffer on both the forward and return path interfaces.

    Expected maxima: q=100 → ~1620 ms (observed ~1518 ms);
                     q=20  → ~340 ms  (observed ~349 ms).

Q4. Identify and describe two ways to mitigate the bufferbloat problem.

    1. Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) — routers mark packets with a
       congestion signal in the IP header instead of dropping them once the
       queue exceeds a threshold. ECN-aware TCP senders reduce their rate before
       the buffer fills, keeping queues shallow without the throughput loss of
       drops.

    2. TCP BBR (Bottleneck Bandwidth and RTT) — instead of reacting to loss,
       BBR probes for available bandwidth and minimum RTT, then paces sends to
       match the bottleneck rate. By avoiding buffer filling altogether, BBR
       keeps standing queues near zero while sustaining high throughput.
